==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|scarf}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*skarpaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*(s)kerb(h)}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*(s)ker-||to cut}}. Compare Low German {{m|nds|scharp}}, Dutch {{m|nl|scherp}}, English {{m|en|sharp}}, Danish {{m|da|skarp}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʃaʁf/|lang=de}} (''standard'')
* {{IPA|/ʃaːf/|lang=de}} (''widespread, esp. northern and central Germany'')
* {{audio|De-scharf.ogg|audio|lang=de}}
* {{homophones|Schaf|lang=de}} {{qualifier|nonstandard}}

